The runtime's own guest memory gets a home of its own in the console's free kernel area

What it fixes: the runtime kept the structures of its own that live in guest RAM - the invocation
stacks of queued guest calls (PS2Runtime::reserveAsyncCallbackStack), the remote-call packet and
server pools, the thread-local-storage pool and the boot-mode pool (Kernel/Syscalls/Helpers/State.h)
- at 0x01F00000 and above, and its scratch allocator behind guestMalloc/guestCalloc/guestRealloc/
guestFree (used by the GS, font and LibC handlers) handed out blocks from the base of the GAME's own
heap, because SetupHeap passed the game's range to configureGuestHeap. Two collisions followed, both
measured in this tree before the change (breakpoints during a 30 s run of the port): the first GS
packet came from 0x5A6800, the base of the game's heap, whose own C library (recompiled, FUN_001270c0
- real code, not a handler) keeps its bookkeeping there and puts its first arena at 0x5A6840; and the
invocation stacks grew down from the top of RAM (0x02000000) over the main thread's stack
(0x01F80000-0x02000000). To leave room for them the runtime clamped the game's heap to 0x01F00000,
where the console's kernel lets it end at 0x01F80000: measured against the console's own memory image
(work/ref/x/01/ee_ram.bin, the title screen; the same in 04 and 06), the game's first heap request of
0x19D73F0 succeeds there - the arena word 0x4D69C4 is 0x9D73F0 (the request minus the first arena of
0x1000000) and 0x4D69B8 is 0x1F50C20 - while in the port it did not (0x4D69C4 = 0x9596F0, 0x7DD00 =
513.5 KiB smaller), so the boot shrank its own arena and every texture pool of the engine landed in
the game's data instead of its heap (journal 006, items 27-30). The console's kernel occupies
0x00080000 to 0x00100000 (measured on all six console images: 0x00080000-0x0009FFFF holds 3902
non-zero 16-byte words, 0x000A0000-0x000FFFFF is all zeroes) and does not exist in this runtime, while
nothing of the game lives below 0x00100000, so that is where the runtime's own memory now goes:

    0x00080000-0x000BFFFF  invocation stacks: 16 of 0x4000, bumped downwards from 0x000C0000, never freed
    0x000C0000-0x000CFFFF  remote-call packet pool (wraps, unchanged behaviour)
    0x000D0000-0x000DFFFF  remote-call server pool (wraps, unchanged behaviour)
    0x000E0000-0x000EFFFF  thread-local-storage pool (wraps, unchanged behaviour)
    0x000F0000-0x000F0FFF  boot-mode pool (does not wrap)
    0x000F1000-0x000FFFFF  the runtime's scratch allocator (guestMalloc and friends)

The layout is defined once, in the new header runtime/ps2_guest_memory.h, with static_asserts that no
region overlaps another and that all of them end at or below 0x00100000; State.h and ps2_runtime.h
include it. SetupHeap and EndOfHeap follow the console's kernel: the end is start + size, or, when the
size is -1 (0xFFFFFFFF), the bottom of the calling thread's stack as SetupThread recorded it (the game
calls SetupThread with stack -1 and size 0x80000, so its stack is the top 0x80000 bytes of RAM and the
heap ends at 0x01F80000); there is no 0x01F00000 clamp any more. SetupHeap's return value is unchanged
(the aligned base): the game's own entry code (0x1001E8) ignores it, checked by decompiling it.
Exhaustion is loud, never silent: the boot-mode pool, the invocation stacks and the runtime's
allocator print one line naming the region and the size asked (the packet, server and TLS pools keep
wrapping by design). The runtime's allocator now answers from 0xF000 bytes, so a handler asking for
more is refused loudly instead of silently: the font handler asks 0x2010 and 0xc400 and CreateThread
asks for a whole stack when the game passes stack 0 (this game passes its own stacks and does not call
the font handlers; both paths were checked with breakpoints). Note for later: this game's C library is
recompiled, so the runtime's malloc family (Kernel/Stubs/LibC.cpp, Compatibility.cpp) is not used by
it; after this patch those handlers allocate from the runtime's private range, so a game that relied on
them for its whole heap would need the previous behaviour.

How it was verified: tests/runtime/guest_heap_limit_test.cpp (new) fails against the tree without this
patch (13 checks: EndOfHeap answers 0x01F00000, the pools 0x01F00000/0x01F10000/0x01F20000/0x01F30000,
the invocation stack 0x01FFBFF0 and the scratch block 0x005A6800, all inside the game's memory) and
passes with it (EndOfHeap 0x01F80000, server 0x000D0080, packet 0x000C0040, tls 0x000E0100, bootmode
0x000F0020, invocation stack 0x000B7FF0, scratch 0x000F1100); the other eight executables of
tests/runtime still pass, built with PS2X_WITH_RAYLIB=OFF and DISPLAY unset. In the game: before the
patch a 30 s run ended at frame 0 with RESULT: MISSING_BRANCH_TARGET 0x0; after it three 60 s runs give
RESULT: OK_VSYNC_LOOP (frames and vsyncs of each run are in docs/journal/006-file-packages.md), the
game boots to its memory-card warning dialog and draws it (--shots work/shots/boot writes
frame_000300.png), the 14 module loads and the remote-call server registrations still happen, and the
RAM dump against work/ref/x/01/ee_ram.bin gives the game's arena words equal to the console's at
0x4D69B8 (0x01F50C20) and 0x4D69C4 (0x009D73F0), the first pool descriptor 0x5446E0 equal in both
fields (0x00D29C00/0x0082A8C0) and 0x544708 equal in +0x14 (0x007A31F0, +0x10 0xE0 away: a different
number of allocations by the end of the run). With breakpoints during a 60 s run: 4 of the 16
invocation stacks are reserved (0x4000 bytes each) and the runtime's allocator serves 2 requests, both
0x80 bytes.

Origin: original work for this project (T-016). The console facts come from the memory images in
work/ref/x/ (captures of the game on the reference emulator, taken by the orchestrator).

